  <h1>Types of Lemon Law Claims</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>State lemon law repurchase</h2><p>The manufacturer buys back the vehicle at the original purchase price (less mileage offset) plus incidental costs (taxes, registration, finance charges). The strongest remedy; most states mandate it once the qualification threshold is met.</p>
  <h2>Replacement vehicle</h2><p>The manufacturer provides a comparable new vehicle in lieu of a cash refund. Some consumers prefer this; others prefer cash. The replacement vehicle must be comparable in class and equipment.</p>
  <h2>Cash settlement</h2><p>The manufacturer pays a cash amount to settle the claim without repurchase. Usually less than the full refund amount but resolved faster and without litigation. Common when the defect is significant but doesn&rsquo;t clearly meet the statutory threshold.</p>
  <h2>Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act claim</h2><p>Federal claim available in addition to state lemon law. Allows consumers to sue for breach of written warranty. Attorney fees mandatory for prevailing plaintiffs. Covers vehicles not covered by state lemon law (used vehicles with written warranties, vehicles outside the state lemon law period).</p>
